Date: Tue, 19 Jan 93 9:38:27 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Restitution is Usually Not Enough

Mark Pickens writes:

> Libertarianism involves non-initiation of force. Once the scale has been
> balanced between criminal and victim we have a clean slate.  If we now tilt
> the scale against the original criminal, WE are initiating force.  By doing
> so, we violate Libertarian theory.  At least, that's how I see it.

So if an arsonist is caught he can simply pay for the damages and then
have a clean slate? Since arson is such an easy crime to get away
with, you'll get a lot of bang for your buck in this system!

The main problem with restitution is that it makes crime a potentially
profitable business over the long run. The "legal credit" idea, which
seems to me to be another form of reputation-based system, is a
possible stopgap, though.

- If you get caught, you pay restitution...and possibly some
court/insurance/etc. costs. Perhaps your reputation suffers (on the
other hand, in your particular subculture, it may be enhanced.)

- If you _don't_ get caught, you pay nothing. And your reputation
doesn't suffer.

This point, that straight restitution may not be enough, has been made
by many people, including David Friedman in "Machinery of Freedom." It
seems to be an open question.

Tom Morrow may be right that the "legal credit" idea may help to solve
this problem. But I have my doubts. 

For example, imagine two cultures, homeowners and thieves. A thief who
gets caught--perhaps in one out of 10 jobs--has to pay restitution (no
big deal to him) AND he sees his "legal credit" diminished. Whoahh!
Now that's a real incentive not to steal, right? (I'll put the sarcasm
aside, as it may not read well...)

In the thief culture, "reps" are made by getting busted
(occasionally). Look at how gang members and Mafia wise guys "make
their bones" by committing crimes. Will a legal credit system deter
them?

It might be argued that the "rest of society" will ostracize them for
their poor legal credit, refusing to deal with them. Etc. But all the
evidence suggests the opposite: hotels continue to court Mafiosos,
nightclubs cater to known underworld figures, and businesses in inner
cities jump through hoops to keep the "homeboys" happy. Ever see the
Bloods and Crips unable to buy food?

Am I arguing against reputation-based systems? No.

What I _am_ arguing is that "disproportionate response" may be
needed--deterrence. Someone breaking into my house to steal my stuff
must be aware that he faces more of a response than, at worst, paying
for the stuff he stole. He may get shot, he may get thrown in prison
(privately funded or whatever), and he may have to pay off 10 or 100
times the value of what he stole or tried to steal.

Friedman acknowledged that he was unhappy with the various formulas
for payback. I consider this to remain an open question.

Consider arson. Some people set fires for kicks, others for financial
gain. The chances of getting caught are low, for obvious reasons
(empty buildings, time delays on incendiary devices are easy to build,
is trivial to thrown gasoline on the side of a house and light it,
etc.).

Now do we really want to have the "libertarian" solution and simply
demand restitution in the 10% (estimated) cases where an arsonist is
caught? And if we force him to pay 10x, to cover this low probability
of being caught, we've already forced him to pay for the crimes of
_others_, so our libertarian principles are violated.

Me, I have no problems with swift and deadly retaliation against a
wide variety of real crimes.


-Tim


