I believe there is a flaw in Mr. Hanson's reasoning about
career choice by neglecting the effect of savings.  I will
use engineering as an example, since I am most familiar with
the numbers in my own field.  The ratio of peak salary to
starting salary is $80K/32K = 2.5:1, and this occurs over
40 years (age 25 to 65), so the compound rate of growth in
income is 2.3% per year.  If we assume that income as
an engineer continued to rise at that rate indefinitely,
the following would happen:

Let us say the engineer saved a modest portion of his
income, 10%, and invested it at 5% real return.  By the
74th year, income from savings would exceed income from
career, both being over 5 times the starting salary.
by the 156th year, income from savings would be 10 times
income from career, which would make career choice pretty
irrelevant for income purposes.

If one saves 30% instead (my own savings rate on average)
then the parity level is achieved in 42 years, and the
savings income = 10x career income point comes in the
115th year.  If you save 30% the first year, and drop
the savings rate by 1% per year, and don't save anything
after the first 30 years, then the parity level is
reached in the 71st year, and the 10x level is reached
in the 160th year.

So, in general, as long as you have some reasonable
savings level, and you can earn a higher growth rate
on your savings than your salary increases, you get
to a point where you can live off your savings, and
career choice becomes a matter of preference, rather
than necessity.  The time to income replacement is
40-75 years, so I would expect a first career to
last on the order of a half century to a maximum of
150 years, and thereafter a person would be doing
what they wanted (possibly staying on, but changing
if that's what they want to do)

One effect of this is what we in the industry call
"short-timer's attitude".  When an engineer gets
within a couple of years of retirement, often he
gets the attitude "I don't care much what my 
raises are in these last couple of years, it won't
make much difference to my retirement pension, so
I don't have to work hard anymore to get raises,
I just have to do enough to keep from getting
fired.  And I don't have to take any bull from
my boss or watch what I say"  If you get a big
population of senior workers who are financially
self-sufficient, the nature of the boss-employee
realtionship will change drastically.

Dani Eder

