1. The detailed numbers seem largely meaningless (sorry, Dani) over
such long time scales (70-100 years) that Dani cites. Expect the
nature of employment, saving, and tax rates to change dramatically
over much shorter time scales. For instance, will Intel or Apple even
_exist_ in 30 years? 

2. Peak salary, or at least a salary that is _very much higher_ than
starting salaries, is reached fairly quickly in many high-growth,
talent-driven industries. Neither Michael Jackson nor I had to wait
long before we were earning a lot (relatively speaking).

(All I'll say about this is that I started at Intel in 1974 at the
then-reasonable salary of $12K and was making Dani's 2.5x that, in
real dollars, in less than 4 years. A few years later I was making,
with bonuses, stock options, etc., more than.....well, a _lot_ more.
This was also the experience of a number of Intel's superstars. As for
the non-superstars, they gradually drifted away, were laid-off, or
moved into dead-end middle management and "Human Relations" types of
jobs.  My fear is that Dani is doing his ultra long-range calculations
mainly for these kinds of non-producing "lifers" rather than for the
Extropian superstars that Tom Morrow is clearly discussing.)

3. The idea of staying in one job, one career, for 60 or 80 years, so
as to "max out" on salary, seems unrealistic. A spreadsheet might show
such a behavior to be reasonable, but few of us will have such
patience.

4. Superstars (and we Extropians are surely superstars, are we not?)
will make their mark early in their careers, often by the time they're
25 years old. Creative energies, charged up intellects, the
willingness to work long hours (evenings spent thinking, etc.), and
sheer boldness are what makes this happen. They often get enough
compensation by the time they're 30 or 35 to retire (:-}), to start
other companies, or to change careers completely.

I'm sorry if my personal experiences are an intrusion into a logical
argument. I'm not bragging...we're all Extropians, anyway, right? But
since Tom M. and Robin H., amongst others, have raised the issue of
multiple careers in a lifetime. I thought my personal background would
be of some interest , even if anecdotal.

